    <section class="narrative-section">
      <h2>Underground Fuel Tanks 101: Why They Exist in the PNW</h2>
      <p>
        Before natural gas pipelines expanded across Seattle, Edmonds, Shoreline, and Lynnwood in the mid-to-late 20th century, heating oil was the standard residential heating fuel across Washington State. Most single-family homes buried a 300 to 1,000-gallon steel fuel tank directly in the yard.
      </p>
      <p>
        Over time, as homes converted to natural gas or modern heat pumps, many of these tanks were disconnected and left underground. Because bare steel rusts when exposed to moist Pacific Northwest soil, an unmonitored buried tank can eventually corrode and leak petroleum into topsoil or groundwater.
      </p>

      <h2>Decommissioning vs. Full Removal: What's the Difference?</h2>
      <p>
        When dealing with an underground heating oil tank in King or Snohomish County, sellers and buyers generally encounter two paths:
      </p>

      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Factor</th>
            <th>In-Place Decommissioning</th>
            <th>Full Tank Removal</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Process</strong></td>
            <td>Pumping out leftover oil, triple-rinsing the tank interior, and filling it with inert foam or slurry.</td>
            <td>Excavating the yard, digging out the steel tank completely, and hauling it away.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Permits & Paperwork</strong></td>
            <td>Requires a local Fire Department decommissioning permit and an official contractor certificate.</td>
            <td>Requires local mechanical/building permits and environmental soil disposal receipts.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Soil Testing</strong></td>
            <td>Soil samples drawn through bottom soil borings during cleaning.</td>
            <td>Direct visual soil inspection and lab soil sample testing from the open pit.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Lender & Buyer Preference</strong></td>
            <td>Widely accepted if backed by an official Fire Department Decommissioning Certificate.</td>
            <td>Gold standard for total peace of mind; eliminates any future buried liability.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

