      <h2>Washington State PLIA $75,000 Grant Program (HOLG)</h2>
      <p>
        One of Washington State's best financial safety nets for homeowners is the <strong>Heating Oil Loan & Grant (HOLG) Program</strong>, administered by the <strong>Pollution Liability Insurance Agency (PLIA)</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Grant Funding Up to $75,000:</strong> Income-qualified homeowners who register their heating oil tank with PLIA can receive up to $75,000 in grant funds to cover tank removal and soil remediation costs.</li>
        <li><strong>No Out-of-Pocket Crisis:</strong> If an old tank leak is discovered during a soil test, PLIA grants can directly reimburse certified environmental contractors to clean up the soil.</li>
        <li><strong>How to Qualify:</strong> The program covers residential properties with active or abandoned heating oil tanks used for space heating. Registration must be filed before major cleanup begins.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Buyer & Seller Action Plans</h2>
      <p><strong>For Homebuyers:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Order a Tank Sweep ($150–$200):</strong> During your inspection window, hire a specialist to scan the yard with ground-penetrating radar to confirm whether an undisclosed buried tank exists.</li>
        <li><strong>Verify Decommissioning Certificates:</strong> Insist on seeing official Fire Department Decommissioning Certificates and soil sample lab reports before waiving contingencies.</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>For Sellers:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Locate Historic Paperwork Early:</strong> Dig up your fire department decommissioning permits and contractor receipts before listing on the MLS.</li>
        <li><strong>Disclose Accurately:</strong> Complete Section 7 (Environmental) on Form 17 accurately to protect yourself from post-closing buyer liability.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

